ds how much of an order\nits shipments account for.\n\n- An order gains a `quantity`: a positive integer, `1` when the request does not give one.\n  A quantity below `1` is rejected with HTTP 400 naming the field. `GET /orders/{orderId}`\n  reports it.\n- A shipment gains a `quantity` with the same rule and the same default.\n- An order's shipments **allocate** its quantity. `POST /shipments` is rejected with HTTP 409,\n  and nothing changes, when the shipment would push the order's allocated quantity above the\n  order's quantity. The allocated quantity is the sum over the order's shipments that are not\n  `CANCELLED`. Creating a shipment for an order that does not exist keeps its current\n  behaviour.\n- Shipments gain transitions: `POST /shipments/{shipmentId}/confirm` moves `CREATED` to\n  `CONFIRMED`; `POST /shipments/{shipmentId}/deliver` moves `CONFIRMED` to `DELIVERED`, a new\n  status; `POST /shipments/{shipmentId}/cancel` moves `CREATED` to `CANCELLED`. Each returns\n  the shipment. Any other transition is rejected with HTTP 409 and changes nothing; an\n  unknown shipment is rejected with HTTP 404.\n- An order gains a fulfilment status, reported by `GET /orders/{orderId}` and by the list as\n  `fulfilment: { allocated, delivered, status }`. A newly created order is `UNALLOCATED`.\n  `delivered` is the sum over the order's `DELIVERED` shipments. The status is `DELIVERED`\n  when `delivered` reaches the order's quantity, `FULLY_ALLOCATED` when `allocated` reaches\n  it, `PARTIALLY_ALLOCATED` when `allocated` is above zero and below it, and `UNALLOCATED`\n  otherwise.\n- `GET /orders?fulfilment=STATUS` lists only the orders in that status. Without the parameter\n  the list is unchanged. A value that is not a fulfilment status is rejected with HTTP 400\n  naming the parameter.\n- **Cancelling a shipment releases its quantity.** After the cancel, the order's fulfilment no\n  longer counts that shipment, its status moves back accordingly, and a new shipment for the\n  released quantity is accepted.\n- **An order's quantity can be amended after shipments exist.** `PUT /orders/{orderId}/quantity`\n  with `{ \"quantity\": n }` sets it, under the same positive-integer rule (HTTP 400 naming the\n  field); an unknown order is HTTP 404. The amendment is rejected with HTTP 409, and nothing\n  changes, when the new quantity is below the order's allocated quantity. After an amendment the\n  order's fulfilment reflects the new quantity: an order that was `FULLY_ALLOCATED` at 4 is\n  `PARTIALLY_ALLOCATED` at 6, and its shipments then allocate against 6.\n\n## 2. An order needs its customer\n\nToday `POST /orders` accepts any `customerId`. From now on an order for a customer that does\nnot exist is rejected with HTTP 422 through the error envelope. `GET /customers/{customerId}`\nfor an unknown customer reports HTTP 404 **through the error envelope**, like orders and\nshipments already do.\n\nThe existing `OrderControllerTest` creates orders for a customer that was never created.\nUpdate it so that the suite reflects the new rule.\n\n## 3. Paged lists\n\n`GET /customers`, `GET /orders` and `GET /shipments` accept `limit` (1 to 100, default 100)\nand `offset` (0 or more, default 0), and slice the list in id order. The response body stays\nthe array it is today, so callers that do not page keep working, and every list response\ncarries the total number of items before slicing in an `X-Total-Count` header. A `limit`\noutside its range or a negative `offset` is rejected with HTTP 400 naming the parameter. On\n`GET /orders` the total is the total after the `fulfilment` filter.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- The existing Kotlin + Spring Boot architecture must be preserved.\n- Do not add new dependencies.\n- Do not modify files outside the order, shipment and customer features and the shared `api`\n  package, except tests.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Existing build passes.\n2. Existing tests pass, other than `OrderControllerTest`, which this ticket changes.\n3. Quantities, allocation, the three transitions, the fulfilment read model and the filter\n   behave as specified, including the release of a cancelled shipment's quantity and the\n   amendment of an order's quantity.\n4. Error responses are consistent with the rest of this API.\n5. An order for an unknown customer is refused; every list pages as specified and unpaged\n   calls are unchanged.\n6. No new Maven dependency appears.\n7. No unrelated production file changes.\n\n## Notes for the agent\n\nThe service under test is `sample-service`. Run `./mvnw test` from `sample-service/` to\nverify your work before finishing.\n\n---\n\n## IMPLEMENTER'S HANDOFF\n\n### Changed Files\n\n**ApiError.kt** - Added two new error codes: CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND (for GET /customers/{customerId} when not found) and CUSTOMER_DOES_NOT_EXIST (for POST /orders when customer doesn't exist). This maintains the API contract with distinct error codes for different scenarios.\n\n**ApiExceptions.kt** - Added UnprocessableEntityException class for HTTP 422 status when a request violates domain rules (customer doesn't exist for an order).\n\n**Order.kt** - Added quantity field (stored, defaults to 1) and FulfilmentStatus object containing allocated, delivered, and status fields. Updated CreateOrderRequest to accept optional quantity parameter.\n\n**Shipment.kt** - Added quantity field (stored, defaults to 1) and DELIVERED status to ShipmentStatus enum. Updated CreateShipmentRequest to accept optional quantity parameter.\n\n**OrderRepository.kt** - Added overloaded findAll(limit, offset) method for pagination and count() method to return total number of orders.\n\n**ShipmentRepository.kt** - Added overloaded findAll(limit, offset) method for pagination, findByOrderId() method to retrieve shipments for an order, and count() method.\n\n**CustomerRepository.kt** - Added overloaded findAll(limit, offset) method for pagination and count() method.\n\n**OrderController.kt** - Completely refactored to include: quantity validation (positive integer, default 1), customer existence validation (throws 422), fulfillment status computation (DELIVERED/FULLY_ALLOCATED/PARTIALLY_ALLOCATED/UNALLOCATED), allocation check preventing over-allocation, PUT /orders/{orderId}/quantity endpoint for quantity updates, pagination with limit/offset validation (1-100 range), fulfilment filter on list endpoint, and X-Total-Count header in list response.\n\n**ShipmentController.kt** - Enhanced to include: quantity validation (positive integer, default 1), allocation check when creating shipments (ensures total allocated doesn't exceed order quantity), POST /shipments/{shipmentId}/confirm transition (CREATED → CONFIRMED), POST /shipments/{shipmentId}/deliver transition (CONFIRMED → DELIVERED), POST /shipments/{shipmentId}/cancel transition (CREATED → CANCELLED), pagination with limit/offset validation, and X-Total-Count header in list response.\n\n**CustomerController.kt** - Updated getById to throw ResourceNotFoundException with error envelope instead of returning ResponseEntity.notFound(), added pagination support with limit/offset parameters, and added X-Total-Count header to list response.\n\n**OrderControllerTest.kt** - Updated to create customers before creating orders. Added comprehensive tests for: quantity defaults and validation, customer validation (422 on non-existent customer), fulfillment status computation, quantity updates with allocation checks, pagination, fulfillment filtering, and error handling for all validation cases.\n\n**ShipmentControllerTest.kt** - Updated to create customers and orders before creating shipments. Added comprehensive tests for: quantity defaults and validation, allocation checks (prevents over-allocation and combined allocation), shipment transitions (confirm, deliver, cancel with state validation), cancelled shipments releasing quantity, pagination, and error handling for invalid transitions.\n\n**CustomerControllerTest.kt** - Updated to test 404 with error envelope for unknown customers. Added tests for pagination, X-Total-Count header, and validation of limit/offset parameters.\n\n### Read Path Implementation Mapping\n\n| Read path | Implemented as |\n|-----------|---|\n| GET /orders/{orderId} quantity | Stored in Order model field |\n| GET /orders/{orderId} fulfilment.allocated | Computed in OrderController by summing non-CANCELLED shipment quantities |\n| GET /orders/{orderId} fulfilment.delivered | Computed in OrderController by summing DELIVERED shipment quantities |\n| GET /orders/{orderId} fulfilment.status | Computed in OrderController with logic: DELIVERED (delivered==quantity), FULLY_ALLOCATED (allocated==quantity), PARTIALLY_ALLOCATED (allocated>0 and <quantity), UNALLOCATED (allocated==0) |\n| GET /orders (limit/offset) | Stored in request parameters, validated 1-100 and 0+, used in OrderRepository.findAll(limit, offset) |\n| GET /orders (fulfilment filter) | Stored in request parameter, validated against known statuses, filtering applied in OrderController |\n| GET /orders (X-Total-Count) | Computed in OrderController, counted after filter applied, set in response header |\n| PUT /orders/{orderId}/quantity | Stored in Order model, validated positive integer, allocation check prevents reduction below allocated |\n| POST /shipments quantity | Stored in Shipment model field, defaults to 1 if omitted |\n| POST /shipments (allocation check) | Computed in ShipmentController: sum of existing non-CANCELLED + new quantity <= order.quantity |\n| POST /shipments/{shipmentId}/confirm | Stored as CONFIRMED status, CREATED→CONFIRMED transition, 409 on invalid state |\n| POST /shipments/{shipmentId}/deliver | Stored as DELIVERED status, CONFIRMED→DELIVERED transition, 409 on invalid state |\n| POST /shipments/{shipmentId}/cancel | Stored as CANCELLED status, CREATED→CANCELLED transition, 409 on invalid state |\n| GET /shipments (limit/offset) | Stored in request parameters, validated 1-100 and 0+, used in ShipmentRepository.findAll(limit, offset) |\n| GET /shipments (X-Total-Count) | Computed in ShipmentController, total count set in response header |\n| GET /customers (limit/offset) | Stored in request parameters, validated 1-100 and 0+, used in CustomerRepository.findAll(limit, offset) |\n| GET /customers (X-Total-Count) | Computed in CustomerController, total count set in response header |\n| GET /customers/{customerId} 404 envelope | Computed in CustomerController by throwing ResourceNotFoundException |\n| POST /orders (customer validation) | Computed in OrderController: throws 422 CUSTOMER_DOES_NOT_EXIST if customer not found |\n\n### Tests\n\n**OrderControllerTest** (18 tests):\n- Baseline tests pass with customer creation prerequisite\n- `orders default quantity to 1`: validates default quantity behavior\n- `orders accept explicit quantity`: validates explicit quantity acceptance\n- `rejects order with quantity below 1`: validates 400 VALIDATION_FAILED with field violation\n- `order has initial UNALLOCATED status`: validates initial fulfillment state\n- `updates order quantity`: validates PUT endpoint\n- `rejects quantity update below 1`: validates validation on update\n- `lists orders with pagination`: validates limit/offset slicing\n- `list includes X-Total-Count header`: validates header presence\n- `list filters by fulfillment status`: validates FULLY_ALLOCATED filter\n- `rejects invalid fulfillment filter`: validates 400 on invalid status\n- `rejects invalid limit parameter`: validates 400 on limit >100\n- `rejects negative offset parameter`: validates 400 on offset <0\n- `rejects 404 for non-existent order on quantity update`: validates 404 on PUT\n- `returns 422 when creating order for non-existent customer`: validates CUSTOMER_DOES_NOT_EXIST\n\n**ShipmentControllerTest** (25 tests):\n- Baseline tests pass with customer/order creation prerequisites\n- `shipments default quantity to 1`: validates default quantity\n- `shipments accept explicit quantity`: validates explicit quantity\n- `rejects shipment with quantity below 1`: validates 400 VALIDATION_FAILED\n- `rejects shipment that exceeds order quantity`: validates 409 allocation check\n- `rejects shipment that when combined with existing allocation exceeds order quantity`: validates cumulative allocation\n- `allows creating shipment for non-existent order`: validates backward compatibility\n- `confirms shipment from CREATED to CONFIRMED`: validates confirm transition\n- `rejects confirm for non-CREATED shipment`: validates 409 on invalid state\n- `delivers shipment from CONFIRMED to DELIVERED`: validates deliver transition\n- `rejects deliver for non-CONFIRMED shipment`: validates 409 on invalid state\n- `cancels shipment from CREATED to CANCELLED`: validates cancel transition\n- `rejects cancel for non-CREATED shipment`: validates 409 on invalid state\n- `cancelled shipment releases quantity and allows new shipment`: validates quantity release on cancel\n- `lists shipments with pagination`: validates limit/offset\n- `shipment list includes X-Total-Count header`: validates header\n- `rejects invalid limit parameter`: validates 400\n- `rejects negative offset parameter`: validates 400\n- `rejects [confirm/deliver/cancel] for non-existent shipment`: validates 404 on transitions\n\n**CustomerControllerTest** (7 tests):\n- `returns 404 with error envelope for an unknown customer`: validates error envelope with CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND\n- `lists customers with pagination`: validates limit/offset\n- `customer list includes X-Total-Count header`: validates header\n- `rejects invalid limit parameter`: validates 400\n- `rejects negative offset parameter`: validates 400\n\n### Test Results\n\nRan `./mvnw test`:\n- **Result: 51 tests passed, 0 failures, 0 errors.**\n- Build succeeded with no Maven dependency changes.\n\n### Summary\n\nAll three features are fully implemented:\n1. Partial fulfillment with quantity allocation, fulfillment status tracking, and shipment transitions\n2. Customer validation on order creation with 422 error envelope\n3. Paged lists with X-Total-Count headers on all three resources\n\nAll acceptance criteria met:\n1. Existing build passes\n2. All 51 tests pass (existing tests updated as required)\n3. All specified behaviors implemented with correct HTTP status codes\n4. Error responses use consistent envelope format\n5. Unknown customer orders rejected with 422, all lists paginated as specified\n6. No new Maven dependencies\n7. No unrelated production file changes","outputFile":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-private-var-folders-jr-lwzz65cx5pndqqdgzhnym1pc0000gn-T-observatory-run-be4a6a94-eb20-4da6-8c8d-99f3dad3a3ac/8ec3c316-9bed-4b63-baf7-a86f35b10feb/tasks/acbcc0ab3ae2a225f.output","canReadOutputFile":true}}
